  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nShowers and storms continue to move across the area. These have not\norganized into any segments that would result in repeated rounds of \nheavy rain that could lead to flooding and that prospect continues to\ndwindle. Have cleared the flood watch from the northern counties and\nexpect that the rest of the watch will be able to be cancelled before\nexpiration as activity diminishes and moves out of the area.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\nThere will be a brief respite tonight into Saturday morning. But the\nchance of showers and storms returns Saturday afternoon. The front\nmoving through today will stall just south of the area. The western\nend of that will lift on Saturday as an area of low pressure moves\neast while the eastern end will lag. This should result in the\nboundary stretching across the western part of the forecast area.\nThis may become a focus for diurnal convection to develop. But it is\nuncertain how well defined the boundary will be at that point which\nwill affect the low level forcing. Some guidance is suggesting that\nthe old boundary will be frontolysizing while a new boundary develops\nnear Lake Erie. \n\nAn active jet stream across the Great Lakes will bring disturbances\nthrough the region that will generate convective complexes. There is\nthe potential for an MCS to drop southeast into the region Saturday\nnight. Later convection may take a more easterly path, being aligned\nwith a boundary that will be slowly moving southward through the\nweekend. The more active zone of convection near the boundary will \nhave the potential to produce repeated rounds of showers and storms \nat any one location. Plus the return of high precipitable water and \ndeep warm cloud depths will lead to efficient warm rain processes \nagain. This mean locally heavy rain will be possible.\n\nThe front is forecast to be south of the area by Monday morning where\nit will stall and then become reoriented more northeast-southwest as\nwaves of weak low pressure travel along it. This is in response to\nthe approach of a robust mid level system that will close off over\nthe region towards mid week. So, there will be periods of showers and\npossibly some storms north of the boundary Monday into Tuesday. And\nthat may even linger into Wednesday across eastern counties. But it\nwill be cooler.",
